# The kinds of C caller's argument token, and their mints
# (verus/count, mints.rs; verus/README.md, "What counts as verified").
#
# {CAPI_CRATE} makes each C caller's argument token through one trusted
# function, `{MINT_FN}`, which makes a token of any kind that implements
# `{TOKEN_TRAIT}`. Each kind stands for a premise, the C caller's
# contract its mint's `# Safety` states, and each function that calls
# `{MINT_FN}` is a kind's mint, verified over it. So each kind and each
# mint is a row here:
#
#   kind FILE KIND SEAM     an `impl {TOKEN_TRAIT} for KIND` in FILE, and
#                           the seam whose row states its premise
#   mint FILE FN KIND       a function of FILE whose whole body is one call
#                           of `{MINT_FN}` (`unsafe { {MINT_FN}(args) }`),
#                           and which returns `Tracked<KIND>`, so the kind
#                           it makes is KIND
#
# KIND is the type with its lifetimes and generic arguments left out
# (`&mut CObj`). verus/count reads every crate's sources and fails on an
# impl or a mint this file does not list, on a mint that returns another
# kind than its row names, on a mint whose body does more than call
# `{MINT_FN}`, on a row it does not find (stale) or finds twice, on
# `{MINT_FN}` named other than in a call, on `{TOKEN_TRAIT}` named other
# than in an item-level impl, the trait, a `use` or a bound (an impl in a
# function body or a `const _` block would not be an item it reads), on a
# `use` that renames `{MINT_FN}` or `{TOKEN_TRAIT}`, on either inside a
# macro other than `verus!`, and on a SEAM no seams table
# (verus/SEAMS.md, verus/theorems/*.md) has.
#
# A port whose C API makes no ghost tokens on trust has no use for this
# file: leave the gate off in the counter's configuration and delete it.
#
# kit: proved-port kit/v{KIT_VERSION}
